 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Possessives before the noun",
     "texto": [
      "Possessive determiners are placed directly before the noun:\n\n- **mi, mis**: mi hermano, mi hermana, mis padres\n- **tu, tus**: tu libro, tus amigos\n- **su, sus**: su casa, sus libros\n- **nuestro, nuestra, nuestros, nuestras**: nuestro profesor, nuestra clase, nuestros libros, nuestras mesas\n\nThe possessives **mi, tu, and su** only change for number (singular or plural), never for gender. In contrast, **nuestro** changes for both gender and number to agree with the noun.\n\nRemember: **tu** (no accent) is the possessive ('your': tu casa), whereas **tú** (with an accent) is the subject pronoun ('you': tú vives aquí).\n\nThe form **su / sus** can mean his, her, its, your (formal usted), or their. If context does not make it obvious, specify the name: *la casa de Ana*."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Agreement of possessives",
     "texto": [
      "Notice how the possessive agrees in gender and number with the item possessed:\n\n- **Masculine singular**: Tu libro está en la mesa.\n- **Femenine singular**: Nuestra clase es por la mañana.\n\nNotice that we say *nuestra clase* because *clase* is feminine, regardless of whether the speakers are male or female."
     ],
